{"name":"Thalassa","hex":"#53b1ba","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Thalassa","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A moon of Neptune.","The primordial goddess of the sea."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Thalassa"}],"entries":[{"title":"Thalassa","blocks":[{"text":"In Greek mythology, Thalassa is a divine female personification of the sea. Her name may have been of Pre-Greek origin and connected to the name of the Mesopotamian primordial sea goddess Tiamat."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Hatay_thalassa.jpg/330px-Hatay_thalassa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thalassa"},{"title":"Thalassa (moon)","blocks":[{"text":"Thalassa, also known as Neptune IV, is the second-innermost satellite of Neptune. Thalassa was named after sea goddess Thalassa, a daughter of Aether and Hemera from Greek mythology. \"Thalassa\" is also the Greek word for \"sea\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5c/Neptune_Trio.jpg/330px-Neptune_Trio.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thalassa_(moon)"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Rackham_shipwreck.jpg/500px-Rackham_shipwreck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Rackham_shipwreck.jpg/500px-Rackham_shipwreck.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Thalassa defends herself in Aesop's fable, \"The Farmer and the Sea\"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARackham_shipwreck.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/30/ViennaDioscoridesCoral.jpg/500px-ViennaDioscoridesCoral.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, htt